= Introduction

Spacecraft have historically followed a distinct design approach compared to
conventional electronics. While the broader electronics industry has experienced
rapid innovation, progress in space electronics has been comparatively slow.
However, in recent decades, there has been a noticeable shift towards
incorporating commercial off-the-shelf parts (COTS) within the space sector
@label2017cots.

The main advantage of COTS parts is their comparatively low cost. Space grade
parts have a limited market size and therefore low production volumes. The result
is that the development costs make up a large portion of the total price per part.
COTS manufacturers on the other hand address a significantly larger market, which
reduces the development cost per part and increases availability @label2017cots.

The governmental space organizations have used aerospace-grade components in the
past due to their detailed knowledge about performance and reliability in their
target environment @label2017cots. CubeSats however were mainly developed by
universities and small companies with comparatively limited resources and
personnel. This required a different engineering approach and the usage of cheap
COTS parts. With the NewSpace era, these principles have become increasingly
widespread @budroweit2021risk.

== Research Question

This thesis investigates the considerations, environmental constraints and
mitigation strategies relevant to the use of COTS parts and small satellite
systems across the different Earth orbits.

== Structure of the Thesis

The remainder of this thesis is organised as follows.
@sec:literature-review reviews the different Earth orbits and their use-cases.
@sec:data-collection describes the space environment and the considerations for
using COTS parts in CubeSats.
@sec:analysis analyses two representative environmental effects, atmospheric drag
and radiation exposure, in more detail.
@sec:conclusions summarises the findings and outlines directions for future work.
